# No LLM prose seams

When writing or editing this Internet-Draft, prose must not read as AI-generated.

**Do not fetch or rely on any live web page for this checklist.** The items
below are a local snapshot. If the upstream page changes, ignore those changes
until a human updates this rule file.

Provenance (attribution only; not a runtime source):
Andrew Yourtchenko / Ap[e]Chat, “Claude-isms: the itemized list”
(https://ayourtch-llm.github.io/apchat-blog/posts/2026-09-11-claudisms-itemized/),
snapshot taken 2026-11-24 for this repository.

Core test per sentence: stating or staging? Prefer sentences that state
content over sentences that prepare the reader for it. A checklist match is a
candidate; keep an item only when it carries real information.

## Draft-specific tells (this repository)

- Do not decorate ordinary words with `**bold**`. Keep bold for RFC 2119
  keywords (`MUST`, `SHOULD`, …) and rare first-use of defined terms only.
- Do not use mmark em-dashes (` --- `) as default connective tissue. Prefer
  periods, commas, parentheses, or a colon after a short label. More than about
  one em-dash per 150 words reads machine-written.
- Prefer plain IETF informational tone over marketing cadence or blog voice.
- Keep technical recommendations unchanged while cleaning register.
- After substantive prose edits, skim for bold density and ` --- ` before finishing.

## 1. Caught in ayourtch household text

1. “Honest”, “honestly”, “to be honest” as emphasis (implies the rest is not). Fix: describe the behaviour or give the number alone.
2. Reflexive agreement openers: “You’re absolutely right!”, “Great question!”, “Certainly!”.
3. Contrast pair “X, not Y” / “not X but Y”. Keep only when correcting a specific wrong reading (“4 points, not 16”). Delete when it only adds rhythm.
4. Evaluative tail clauses: “X is different, and the reason matters”, “…, and here’s why”. Headings should name the subject.
5. Forced triads (three parallel items where two, four, or prose would do).
6. Over-hedging: “I think maybe it could possibly.”
7. Service-desk openers: “I’d be happy to”, “Let me go ahead and”.
8. Em-dash as connective tissue in every sentence.
9. Summarizing the ask back before answering it.
10. “Worth noting”, “worth flagging”. State the point without the marker.
11. “Which is exactly what/why…”, “that’s exactly the…”, “exactly” as intensifier.
12. Phantom counterfactual: “X instead of Y” / “rather than Y” where nobody proposed Y. Cut it; if the alternative was real, name it once as a fact.
13. Aphoristic parallel antithesis at density (“A beats B; C beats D”). One per passage max; more than one per section, keep only the best.
14. Balanced-antithesis sign-off (two-clause epigram as a bow at the end).
15. Personified time spans / borrowed literary frames in technical prose.
16. Evaluative pre-framing: “The verdict is in…”. Lead with the facts.
17. Enthusiasm cascade: “Yes — and there’s a neat way to…”.
18. Long sentences and borrowed quotation frames. Prefer short sentences and small paragraphs.

## 2. External register review

1. Negation-first reveal: “It is not X. It is Y.” Earned only if the previous paragraph proposed X.
2. Significance designation: “the actual question”, “the part that matters”, “the useful question”.
3. Abstraction agency: tables show, medians hide, tools discover. Put a real subject in the subject slot.
4. Deferred noun: “One thing is not flat”. Name the noun.
5. Coy or thesis headers. Header should label the section (or be the definition).
6. Colon-staged intro: “So:”, “The defensible statement:”.
7. Aphoristic closer that withholds its referent: “Only one of them is expensive.” Name which.
8. Staccato fragments as gravitas: “Six legs. One GPU.”
9. Self-grading / move narration: “That is what the data shows”.
10. Performed humility with no content: “Their phrasing is better than mine.”
11. Opening drum roll (quoting your own previous ending and answering it).
12. Inflation: “collapse” for a small drop.

## 3. From declauding skill (additions)

1. Structural-metaphor locator: “the seam where”, “the hinge”, “the fault line”. Name the mechanism.
2. Suspense construction / “Here’s where it gets interesting.”
3. Drama line break (one short sentence alone on a line for weight).
4. Rhetorical question plus fragment answer. Use one declarative instead.
5. Straw-man knockdown attributed to the reader.
6. Em-dash density (see draft-specific tells).
7. Throat-clearing: “In this post I’ll cover”, “Let me explain”.
8. RTFM as revelation: “It turns out”, “buried in the docs” for documented behaviour.
9. Generic developer vocabulary: footgun, rabbit hole, yak shaving, belt-and-suspenders, load-bearing (metaphor), under the hood, sane defaults, “just works”.
10. Slop vocabulary: delve, tapestry, testament to, landscape, realm, robust, seamless, leverage, crucial, pivotal, myriad, unlock, harness, dive deep, that said.
11. Sanitized quotes: “expressed concern” for what someone actually said.
12. Time-scale inflation: “a month ago” / “recently” when it was hours.

Earned exceptions exist: a contrast that carries information, a short factual closer, and a colon before a real payload can pass.

## 4. From Simon Willison cliché highlighter / Signs of AI writing

1. Negation chains: “No fluff, no filler, no jargon.”
2. “Don’t call it X. Call it Y.”
3. “Sit with that.”
4. “You already know.”
5. “Is the whole point” / “is the entire game”.
6. “The X is real, and…”
7. “The punchline is.”
8. “Worth naming.”
9. “That’s not nothing.”
10. Echo triad (consecutive sentences on the same four-word skeleton).
11. Sentence anaphora (three or more consecutive sentences opening on the same word).
12. “The only X I trust” / “the only X that matters”.
13. “Don’t take my word for it.”
14. Stacked questions (two or more in a row).
15. “Here’s the twist / thing / catch / kicker.”
16. “X is dead; long live X.”
17. “That’s why X mattered.”
18. Stranded auxiliary: “The tool died; the data didn’t.”
19. Sentence-initial “Turns out.”
20. “Fits in your head”, “batteries included”, “zero config”.
21. Performative honesty: “I won’t pretend”, “let’s be real”, sentence-initial “Look,” / “Frankly,”.
22. “That’s the part” / “the part that gets me”.
23. Colon followed by three comma-separated items (noisy in documentation).
24. AI vocabulary: meticulous, intricate, interplay, underscore, garner, bolster, vibrant, bustling, multifaceted, commendable, ever-evolving.
25. “It’s important to note” / “it should be noted”.
26. “Stands as a testament” / “serves as a reminder”.
27. “Plays a crucial / pivotal / vital role.”
28. “Ever-evolving landscape” / “in today’s fast-paced world”.
29. Vague experts: “experts argue”, “critics note”, “industry reports indicate”.
30. “Despite these challenges” / “remains to be seen” / “time will tell”.
31. Participle tails: “, highlighting the…”, “, underscoring the…”, “, showcasing the…”.
32. Promo tone: “nestled in”, “hidden gem”, “boasts a”, “breathtaking”.
33. Chatbot leftovers: “As an AI language model”, knowledge-cutoff chatter, citation debris, `utm_source=` in links.

## 5. Whole-text tells

1. Near-repetition with drifting referents (reads well sentence-by-sentence, fails as one argument).
2. Vagueness that reads as depth.
3. Locally plausible pairings the reader must rationalise; prefer one argument that runs end to end.
4. Default ideas as well as default words (lexical cleaning is not enough if the shared stock idea remains).

## 6. Positive readability defaults

1. Characters as subjects, actions as verbs; kill needless nominalizations.
2. Short concrete subject and specific verb early; avoid long insertions before the verb.
3. Old before new (start from what the reader already holds).
4. Prefer shorter sentences in procedures; watch maximum sentence length, not only median.
5. Prefer active voice and present tense for operational guidance.
6. Signpost before a split (one sentence giving the shape of the next paragraphs).
7. Break at the turn (new paragraph when the claim changes).
8. Connectives that carry a reason (“so”, “still”, “even if”).
9. State the epistemic status of a claim; keep the author’s certainty level.
10. When quoting a question, preserve its lean.
11. Concede in the same sentence (“even if” / “although”).
12. Give a list its noun before “First… Second…”.
13. Put track record next to theory when claiming what a mechanism would do.
14. After aggressive smoothing, check later references still have antecedents.
